{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_22-44-04-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nd={\\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Interpreted the failing-snapshot receipt and replaced the constructor question's remaining reachability limitation with that observed path. Receipt 49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193 shows the node's snapshot attempts failing four times and being tolerated, the node's last snapshot never advancing, about 8300 committed entries accumulating above it, and the feature-on restart then never finishing while the same stores restarted without the feature construct and stay at the snapshot index; the fixture's expected batch count exceeded the observed channel capacity. I recorded the tolerated-failure behaviour with a source anchor and the receipt, and updated the question account.\\\",\n \\\"sources\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"s-snapfail\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"snapshot.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":69,\\\"end_line\\\":95,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"}],\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[\n  {\\\"ref\\\":\\\"@snapshot_failures_tolerated\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"behavior\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"The snapshot loop treats a failed snapshot creation as a logged error and keeps running: when takeSnapshot returns an error other than ErrNothingNewToSnapshot the loop logs it and continues, so a node whose snapshot store keeps failing retains its last successful snapshot and its log grows without bound relative to it.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Running leader or follower with a caller-provided SnapshotStore whose Create fails; observed through a wrapper store whose Create fails after the node's first snapshot, with four failed attempts while thousands of entries were committed.\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-snapfail\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193\\\":[0]}},\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"8001bad80a4d4ef0a840634644afdf63-knowledge-0\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"fact\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"With Config.RestoreCommittedLogs enabled and a CommitTrackingLogStore, NewRaft cannot return when the replay must hand more batches to fsmMutateCh than that channel's 128-slot buffer holds, because the only consumer of the channel is runFSM, which NewRaft starts after the replay returns; the number of batches is ceil(committed-but-unapplied entries / MaxAppendEntries), so 129 entries above the last applied index stall the constructor with MaxAppendEntries 1 and 8193 entries do so with the default 64. Entries that never reach the FSM commit path (LogNoop) are skipped by prepareLog and do not create batches.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Construction of a node whose log store reports a commit index above its snapshot/applied index while the feature is enabled; observed with in-memory stores on harness-supplied stores and on live three-node clusters, the latter with MaxAppendEntries 1 and 250 entries, with the default 64 and about 8300 entries reached by delaying snapshots, and with the default 64 and about 8300 entries reached by a snapshot store that fails every attempt (failures logged and tolerated).\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-restorecommitted\\\",\\\"s-processlogs\\\",\\\"s-fsmch\\\",\\\"s-startup-order\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"00386ad8ea534d91a0ef7506fabc9527\\\":[3,7,1,5],\\\"ea93eea3cc08408ab3cd208ca9b1e5ca\\\":[1,3],\\\"e0d98aadc54b461792883fc8a2a82676\\\":[1,3],\\\"49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193\\\":[1,3]},\n   \\\"changed_premise\\\":\\\"\\\"}],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[\n  {\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"e99a162d27c7421fa6729d8cb9703581-question-1\\\",\\\"experiment_id\\\":\\\"0a9d8e8c59184873aa578a5e25adb7dd-experiment\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-restorecommitted\\\",\\\"s-processlogs\\\",\\\"s-fsmch\\\",\\\"s-startup-order\\\",\\\"s-snapfail\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193\\\":[1,3,2,0]},\n   \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"The declared predicate is that a follower whose snapshot attempts all failed and whose staged commit index is more than 128*64 above its last snapshot still finishes NewRaft; the witness contradicts it. Admission index 0 records the live fixture: cluster {A,B,C}, leader B, snapshot index 6, staged commit index 8305, 8299 entries to replay, 130 expected batches at the default MaxAppendEntries 64, the gap flag true, and four failed snapshot-creation attempts tolerated by the library. The feature-on restart at index 1 never returned within 5s; the control restart at index 3 returned with last_applied 6, i.e. still at the snapshot index, and reported the observed capacity 128 against the same demand. Cluster status at index 2 confirms the other two voters kept a leader. Mechanism: the snapshot loop logs a failed Create and continues (snapshot.go runSnapshots), so the node's last snapshot never advances while the commit index does; the restart then replays the accumulated entries through processLogs into fsmMutateCh before NewRaft starts runFSM. Limits: the failing store is a harness wrapper rather than a production store and only the Create failure mode is injected; non-return is observed over a bounded window with permanence argued from source; stores are in-memory, the shutdown graceful and the stalled constructor abandoned.\\\",\n   \\\"limitations\\\":[\\\"The failing snapshot store is a harness wrapper and only Create failures are injected.\\\",\n    \\\"Non-return is observed over a bounded 5s window; permanence rests on the source argument about the missing consumer.\\\",\n    \\\"In-memory stores and a graceful shutdown; the stalled constructor is abandoned without supervision.\\\"],\n   \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\\\"The control restart over the same stores returned and stayed at the snapshot index, so the gap and the stores alone do not explain the stall.\\\",\n    \\\"The fixture records that the snapshot failures were tolerated rather than fatal, so the node kept participating while its snapshot fell behind.\\\"]},\n  {\\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"e99a162d27c7421fa6729d8cb9703581-question-1\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-restorecommitted\\\",\\\"s-processlogs\\\",\\\"s-fsmch\\\",\\\"s-startup-order\\\",\\\"s-snapfail\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193\\\":[1,3,2,0],\n    \\\"e0d98aadc54b461792883fc8a2a82676\\\":[1,3,2,0],\n    \\\"ea93eea3cc08408ab3cd208ca9b1e5ca\\\":[1,3,2,0],\n    \\\"00386ad8ea534d91a0ef7506fabc9527\\\":[3,7,1,5,2,4],\n    \\\"2d92d633d32a4a7c9e204b7dc5c8828b\\\":[0,1,2,3]},\n   \\\"joint_history\\\":\\\"Five receipts over one question and one mechanism. 2d92d633d32a4a7c9e204b7dc5c8828b is retained as an identity failure. 00386ad8ea534d91a0ef7506fabc9527 witnesses the stall on harness-supplied stores at two scales with feature-off controls. ea93eea3cc08408ab3cd208ca9b1e5ca reaches it in a live cluster with MaxAppendEntries 1; e0d98aadc54b461792883fc8a2a82676 repeats that with the default 64 and about 8300 entries while snapshots were merely delayed; 49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193 repeats it once more with the default 64 and a snapshot store that fails every attempt. They share the same cluster shape, fixture construction and control design, and the stall condition (ceil(replay entries / MaxAppendEntries) exceeding the 128-slot channel) is the same in each; each receipt's comparison outcomes are read from that receipt alone.\\\",\n   \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"explained\\\",\\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Answer: yes, and the trigger is now operationally realistic rather than harness-arranged. Snapshot failures are logged and tolerated by the snapshot loop (snapshot.go runSnapshots, receipt 49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193 index 0 records four tolerated failures), so a node whose snapshot store keeps failing keeps its last successful snapshot while its persisted commit index advances; when the accumulated entries exceed 128*MaxAppendEntries the restart cannot finish, because restoreFromCommittedLogs replays them through processLogs into fsmMutateCh before NewRaft starts the only consumer runFSM. At the default batch size the requirement is 8193 entries, just above the default SnapshotThreshold, so a node needs failing or delayed snapshots to reach it - both are now observed (receipt e0d98aadc54b461792883fc8a2a82676 for delay, 49444692e9db40dd84b1aa3c6d603193 for failure). Remaining limits: the failing store is a harness wrapper and only Create failures are injected; non-return is observed over a bounded window with permanence argued from source; stores are in-memory, the shutdown graceful and no supervision, timeout or restart policy is modelled.\\\",\n   \\\"limitations\\\":[\\\"The failing snapshot store is a harness wrapper and only Create failures are injected; a production store's failure modes or a disk-full style failure are not covered.\\\",\n    \\\"Non-return is observed over a bounded 5s window in each receipt; permanence rests on the source argument about the missing consumer.\\\",\n    \\\"In-memory stores and a graceful shutdown; the stalled constructor is abandoned rather than resumed and no supervision, timeout or restart policy is modelled.\\\",\n    \\\"The earlier receipt 2d92d633d32a4a7c9e204b7dc5c8828b remains an identity failure that establishes nothing.\\\"],\n   \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\\\"Feature-off controls over the identical stores construct normally in every receipt, so the gap and the stores alone do not explain the stall.\\\",\n    \\\"No-op entries never reach the FSM commit path and do not stall construction, which scopes the defect to command or barrier entries.\\\",\n    \\\"In the live receipts the other voters kept a quorum, so the defect is a node-level startup failure rather than a cluster-wide outage at that size.\\\"]}]}\njson.dump(d,open('update.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('written')\nPY\ncd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_22-44-04-hashicorp_raft-real-run && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_22-44-04-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -3\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
